  "body": "Follow-up of #2727 (PR #2788).\n\n## What happens\n\n`xtask/src/budget.rs` sets the gate's job budget to a fixed `DEFAULT` of 6 when `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS` is not set. The batch and release tiers split that budget over at most 3 lanes. On a big machine most cores then stay idle.\n\nMeasured on the post-merge runner wopr (Ryzen 9 5950X, 16 cores / 32 threads, 125 GB), head `1da2bcca2` of #2788, warm cache, all green, 929 steps:\n\n| Budget | Batch wall time |\n|---|---|\n| serial (before #2788) | 73 min |\n| default 6 | 30.8 min (load ~15 of 32) |\n| `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=32` | **14.4 min** |\n\nToday every runner needs `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS` in its `.env` (wopr 32, uma 16, m3 16, len 8 are set by hand), and a contributor's machine never gets more than 6.\n\n## Wanted\n\n- When `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS` is not set, the default budget is the number of available CPUs (`std::thread::available_parallelism()`), with a floor of 2 and an upper cap (for example 64).\n- Optionally a memory guard: no more parallel test lanes than the free memory allows (each heavy suite needs ~2-4 GB), so a 32-thread box with 16 GB does not swap.\n- The lane count follows the budget (today at most 3 lanes); the `ISOLATED` steps, builds, ui steps and clippy still run alone with the whole budget.\n- `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS` still overrides; the plan (`--dry-run`) prints the chosen budget and where it came from.\n- AGENTS.md / the `gate` skill say the default is now the core count (process change: PROCESS_VERSION bump at merge).\n\n## Done means\n\nOn a machine without the variable, `cargo xtask gate batch --dry-run` shows a budget equal to the core count (within the cap and memory guard); a unit test pins the default (mock CPU and memory values), the override and the floor/cap; the existing lane tests stay green.\n",
